1.d4 {Welcome to our third and final study of the Beefeater. This chapter will be important when you face experienced and aggressive players. This chapter focuses on the direct and aggressive lines against the Beefeater. Study up!} 1...g6 2.c4 {White puts a second pawn in the center. Let's place our bishop on g7.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {This is a standard approach by White. We can immediately initiate contact in the center.} 3...c5 4.d5 {White plays d5. Let's take the knight.} 4...Bxc3+ 5.bxc3 {White recaptures, damaging their pawn structure. Let's gain some space in the center.} 5...f5 6.e4 {However, what if White plays e4 anyway? First of all, no reason not to trade on e4. We played ...f5 with the intention of disallowing White to keep a pawn on e4.} 6...fxe4 7.f3 {White potentially gives up any hopes of winning the pawn back, and strives for rapid development. We won't bite and let them get the knight out. Let's advance in the center instead.} 7...e5 8.dxe6 {En passant is often played. This isn't losing for White. We can just proceed dynamically by developing our knight. We don't need to recapture and let White trade the queens.} 8...Nc6 9.Be3 {White attacks c5. In that case, we will just defend it with ...d6. The e6-pawn isn't going anywhere.} 9...d6 10.Qd2 {White develops. The pawn is ours to take now.} 10...Bxe6 {You'll notice White's development is hindered by the move f3 as the knight would've loved to go there. For the near future, ...Qh4+ could be an immediate idea to further worsen White's pawn structure. Apart from that, we will develop the knight to f6 and castle.} *

