1.d4 {Welcome to our third and final study of the Beefeater. This chapter will be important when you face experienced and aggressive players. This chapter focuses on the direct and aggressive lines against the Beefeater. Study up!} 1...g6 2.c4 {White puts a second pawn in the center. Let's place our bishop on g7.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {This is a standard approach by White. We can immediately initiate contact in the center.} 3...c5 4.d5 {White plays d5. Let's take the knight.} 4...Bxc3+ 5.bxc3 {White recaptures, damaging their pawn structure. Let's gain some space in the center.} 5...f5 6.e4 {However, what if White plays e4 anyway? First of all, no reason not to trade on e4. We played ...f5 with the intention of disallowing White to keep a pawn on e4.} 6...fxe4 7.f3 {White potentially gives up any hopes of winning the pawn back, and strives for rapid development. We won't bite and let them get the knight out. Let's advance in the center instead.} 7...e5 8.fxe4 {This move has been played many times. It allows us to give a check on h4.} 8...Qh4+ 9.Kd2 {White avoids losing the pawn with check.} 9...Qxe4 {But the pawn is ours either way. From a human standpoint, I would argue this position is better for Black.} *